<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Li Shi (Cheng-Han)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 361</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Li Shi was the last ruler of Cheng-Han, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixteen-kingdoms">Sixteen Kingdoms</a> state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a>, reigning from 343 to 347. The son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-shou">Li Shou</a>, he was unpopular and faced revolts. In 347 the Eastern Jin general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huan-wen">Huan Wen</a> marched up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>, defeated his forces and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu</a>. Li Shi surrendered and was taken to the Jin capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiankang">Jiankang</a>, where he lived out his life.</p></div>
